      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.235,
            0.087,
            0.888,
            0.123
          ],
          "content": "3. As per Eq. (4.2a), by dividing the change in position (BC) by the change in time (CA), you get the average velocity"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.347,
            0.126,
            0.488,
            0.164
          ],
          "content": "\\nu = \\frac{s_{2} - s_{1}}{t_{2} - t_{1}} = \\frac{\\overline{BC}}{CA}",
          "image_source": "images/page_12_equation_1.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.235,
            0.168,
            0.888,
            0.205
          ],
          "content": "4. By extracting values of time $t _ { 1 }$ and $t _ { 2 } ,$ and distances $S _ { 1 }$ and $S _ { 2 }$ from the graph, the magnitude of average velocity can be calculated as"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.354,
            0.206,
            0.658,
            0.243
          ],
          "content": "v=\\frac{80\\  m -40\\  m }{4\\  s -2\\  s }=\\frac{40\\  m }{2\\  s }=20\\  m \\  s ^{-1}",
          "image_source": "images/page_12_equation_3.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.223,
            0.246,
            0.888,
            0.282
          ],
          "content": "From a position-time graph, by finding the positions of the object at two instants of time, you can calculate the average velocity by using Eq. (4.2a)."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.253,
            0.282,
            0.889,
            0.317
          ],
          "content": "Geometrically, $\\frac { \\mathrm { B C } } { \\mathrm { C A } }$ is called the slope of line AB connecting initial"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.223,
            0.317,
            0.89,
            0.37
          ],
          "content": "position A and final position B in Fig. 4.14. The slope of a line is the steepness of the line. The slope of a graph gives information about the rate of change of the quantity shown on Y-axis with respect to the quantity shown on X-axis."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.084,
            0.364,
            0.144,
            0.437
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_12_image_7.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.374,
            0.361,
            0.393
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Ready to Go Beyond"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.142,
            0.396,
            0.815,
            0.431
          ],
          "content": "For the case of a curve also, the velocity at any instant can be calculated geometrically from the position-time graph. You will learn how to do it in higher grades."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.816,
            0.392,
            0.875,
            0.431
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_12_image_10.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "chart",
          "bbox": [
            0.045,
            0.447,
            0.226,
            0.561
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_12_chart_11.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.236,
            0.452,
            0.888,
            0.488
          ],
          "content": "**Example 4.6:** What does the graph shown in Fig. 4.15 indicate about the nature of motion of the vehicle?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.236,
            0.489,
            0.89,
            0.576
          ],
          "content": "**Answer:** The position of the vehicle is 40 m from the origin and is not changing with time. Thus, the vehicle is at rest at 40 m from the origin. A straight line parallel to the time axis on a position-time graph represents a stationary object (In this case, the position-time graph is not the distance-time graph)."
        },
        {
          "type": "chart",
          "bbox": [
            0.216,
            0.689,
            0.472,
            0.877
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.052,
                0.576,
                0.218,
                0.606
              ],
              "content": "Fig. 4.15: Position-time graph of a vehicle"
            },
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.235,
                0.578,
                0.89,
                0.685
              ],
              "content": "**Example 4.7:** The position-time graphs of two objects A and B are given in Fig. 4.16a. The magnitude of average velocity of which object is higher? **Answer:** By making lines parallel to axes as shown in Fig. 4.16b, it is found that the displacement of object B is more than object A for the same time interval. That is, the slope of line for B is steeper than the slope for line A. Thus, the velocity of B is higher than that of A."
            },
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.239,
                0.889,
                0.493,
                0.918
              ],
              "content": "Fig. 4.16(a): Position-time graph of two objects"
            }
          ],
